---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Instagram unfollowers checker: who does not follow you back, checked privately"
  description="See who you follow that does not follow you back, using your own Instagram data export. The file is read inside your browser and never uploaded, no login, no password."
>
  <h1>Instagram unfollowers checker</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every other checker wants your Instagram password. This one does not. It reads the data
    export Instagram itself gives you, inside your browser, and compares the two lists. The
    file never leaves your device. There is nothing here to log into.
  </p>

  <div class="sheet">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0;">The three steps</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>1.</strong> In Instagram, go to Settings, then Accounts Centre, then “Your
      information and permissions”, then “Download your information”. Ask for the followers
      and following lists, in <strong>JSON</strong> format, for the shortest date range that
      covers now. It arrives as a ZIP file, usually within a day.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>2.</strong> Choose that ZIP here. <strong>3.</strong> Read the lists. That is
      all. If the words “upload your file to our server” ever appear on a page like this,
      close it. On this page the reading happens below, in your browser.
    </p>
    <label for="zip">Your Instagram export (ZIP file)</label>
    <input type="file" id="zip" accept=".zip,application/zip" />
    <div id="out" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <p class="result-note">No file chosen yet. Nothing has been sent anywhere, and nothing will be.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What one export can and cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    Be precise about this, because most pages in this corner of the internet are not. One
    export shows who follows you today and who you follow today. Comparing them tells you who
    <strong>does not follow you back</strong>. It cannot tell you who unfollowed you last
    Tuesday, because nothing in the file records when anyone left.
  </p>
  <p>
    To catch actual unfollowers, you need two exports at two times. This page keeps a
    baseline, the follower list from your last visit, stored only in your own browser. Bring a
    fresh export next month and it will name the accounts that were following you then and are
    not now. That is the honest version of the feature, and it is free here, because it costs
    nothing to do properly.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>Can Instagram ban me for using this?</dt>
    <dd>
      There is nothing to ban. You are reading your own data file on your own device. The
      tools that get accounts banned are the ones you give your password to, which log in as
      you and scrape. Never give any website or app your Instagram password for a follower
      list. Turn on two factor authentication while you are thinking about it.
    </dd>
    <dt>Why do other sites claim to show unfollowers from one file?</dt>
    <dd>
      Because “unfollowers” is the word people search for. What they show is the
      does-not-follow-back list, renamed. Some also say the analysis is done locally while
      quietly uploading your file to their server. You can check any page, including this one:
      turn your Wi-Fi off after it loads and it still works.
    </dd>
    <dt>What if my export is in HTML, not JSON?</dt>
    <dd>
      This page reads the HTML files too, but JSON is the better ask: it is smaller and the
      lists come out cleaner. If Instagram gave you folders of HTML pages, the results here
      may miss accounts the JSON would have caught.
    </dd>
    <dt>What is stored, exactly?</dt>
    <dd>
      Only the baseline: a list of usernames that followed you, plus the date, kept in your
      browser’s local storage on this device, so the next export can be compared with it. It
      is never transmitted. Clear your browser data and it is gone, and the comparison simply
      starts again from the next export.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="meta">Method: Instagram data export parsed locally with JSZip; followers compared against following as sets. Page last checked 13 November 2026. Not affiliated with Instagram or Meta.</p>

  <script>
    import JSZip from 'jszip';

    const out = document.getElementById('out');
    const BASE_KEY = 'calquna_ig_baseline';

    function esc(s) {
      return s.replace(/[&<>"']/g, (c) => ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;', "'": '&#39;' }[c]));
    }

    function namesFromJson(data, into) {
      const walk = (node) => {
        if (!node || typeof node !== 'object') return;
        if (Array.isArray(node)) { node.forEach(walk); return; }
        if (Array.isArray(node.string_list_data)) {
          for (const item of node.string_list_data) {
            let name = item && item.value;
            if (!name && item && item.href) name = item.href.split('/').filter(Boolean).pop();
            if (name) into.add(String(name).toLowerCase());
          }
        }
        Object.values(node).forEach(walk);
      };
      walk(data);
    }

    function namesFromHtml(text, into) {
      const re = /instagram\.com\/([A-Za-z0-9._]+)/g;
      let m;
      while ((m = re.exec(text))) {
        const name = m[1].toLowerCase();
        if (!['p', 'reel', 'reels', 'stories', 'explore', 'accounts', 'direct'].includes(name)) into.add(name);
      }
    }

    function listHtml(title, names, note) {
      if (!names.length) return '<h3>' + title + '</h3><p>' + note + '</p>';
      const items = names.map((n) => '<tr><td><a href="https://www.instagram.com/' + esc(n) + '/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@' + esc(n) + '</a></td></tr>').join('');
      return '<h3>' + title + ' (' + names.length + ')</h3><table><tbody>' + items + '</tbody></table>';
    }

    document.getElementById('zip').addEventListener('change', async (ev) => {
      const file = ev.target.files[0];
      if (!file) return;
      out.innerHTML = '<p class="result-note">Reading the file here in your browser…</p>';
      try {
        const zip = await JSZip.loadAsync(file);
        const followers = new Set();
        const following = new Set();
        const pending = new Set();
        let found = 0;
        for (const path of Object.keys(zip.files)) {
          if (zip.files[path].dir) continue;
          const base = path.split('/').pop().toLowerCase();
          let target = null;
          if (base.includes('pending')) target = pending;
          else if (base.startsWith('following')) target = following;
          else if (base.startsWith('followers')) target = followers;
          else continue;
          found++;
          const text = await zip.files[path].async('string');
          if (base.endsWith('.json')) {
            try { namesFromJson(JSON.parse(text), target); } catch { /* skip a file that is not clean JSON */ }
          } else if (base.endsWith('.html') || base.endsWith('.htm')) {
            namesFromHtml(text, target);
          }
        }
        if (!found || (!followers.size && !following.size)) {
          out.innerHTML = '<p class="result-note">That ZIP does not look like an Instagram followers export. Check that the download included "Followers and following" and, if you can choose, ask again in JSON format.</p>';
          return;
        }
        const notBack = [...following].filter((n) => !followers.has(n)).sort();
        const fans = [...followers].filter((n) => !following.has(n)).sort();
        const mutuals = [...followers].filter((n) => following.has(n)).length;

        let html =
          '<p class="result-figure">' + notBack.length + ' do not follow you back</p>' +
          '<p>You follow ' + following.size + ' accounts. ' + followers.size + ' accounts follow you. ' + mutuals + ' of them are mutual.</p>';

        let baselineNote = '';
        try {
          const saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(BASE_KEY) || 'null');
          if (saved && Array.isArray(saved.followers) && saved.followers.length) {
            const before = new Set(saved.followers);
            const gone = [...before].filter((n) => !followers.has(n)).sort();
            const gained = [...followers].filter((n) => !before.has(n)).length;
            html += '<h3>Since your last export (' + esc(saved.date || '') + ')</h3>' +
              '<p>' + gone.length + ' accounts that followed you then are not in this export' + (gained ? ', and ' + gained + ' new followers arrived' : '') + '.</p>';
            if (gone.length) {
              html += '<table><tbody>' + gone.map((n) => '<tr><td>@' + esc(n) + '</td></tr>').join('') + '</tbody></table>';
            }
            baselineNote = 'Your baseline has been updated to this export, so the next one compares against today.';
          } else {
            baselineNote = 'This export is now saved as your baseline in this browser. Bring the next export and this page will name the accounts that left in between.';
          }
          localStorage.setItem(BASE_KEY, JSON.stringify({ date: new Date().toLocaleDateString('en-GB'), followers: [...followers] }));
        } catch {
          baselineNote = 'Your browser would not store the baseline, so the since-last-time comparison is off for now. Everything else above still stands.';
        }
        html += '<p class="result-note">' + baselineNote + '</p>';
        html += listHtml('Does not follow you back', notBack, 'Everyone you follow follows you back.');
        html += listHtml('Fans: follow you, you do not follow them', fans, 'No one-sided fans. You follow back everyone who follows you.');
        if (pending.size) html += listHtml('Follow requests you sent that are still pending', [...pending].sort(), '');
        out.innerHTML = html;
      } catch {
        out.innerHTML = '<p class="result-note">That file could not be read as a ZIP. If Instagram sent you a folder instead, zip the folder first and try again.</p>';
      }
    });
  </script>
</Base>
